   "text": {
    "L1": "Cities are divided into wards so people can take part in local government.",
    "L2": "Myth: keeping the city clean is only the municipality's job. Citizens have no role.",
    "L3": "The people in each ward elect a councillor, also called a corporator, to represent them.",
    "L4": "The elected councillors form a council led by the mayor. An appointed municipal commissioner runs the day-to-day administration.",
    "L5": "Municipal bodies provide water, collect garbage, manage drains, roads, streetlights, parks and public health.",
    "L6": "These services need money. It comes mainly from local taxes and fees, along with grants from state and central governments.",
    "L7": "Citizens help by paying taxes, voting, reporting problems and separating wet and dry waste.",
    "L8": "When citizens and the municipal body work together, the city works better. Now choose the representative elected by a ward."
   },
